{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '77,86p' paper.tex; sed -n '109,114p' paper.tex; sed -n '246,263p' paper.tex; sed -n '335,368p' paper.tex; jq -r '.references[] | [.citation_key // .key // \\\"\\\", .title // \\\"\\\", (.consulted_content // .content_consulted // \\\"\\\" | tostring)] | @tsv' references_provenance.json | head -32; rg -n 'support|fail|unscor|attempt_03|cycle03' paper_evidence_audit_packet.md | head -25\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '77,86p' paper.tex; sed -n '109,114p' paper.tex; sed -n '246,263p' paper.tex; sed -n '335,368p' paper.tex; jq -r '.references[] | [.citation_key // .key // \\\"\\\", .title // \\\"\\\", (.consulted_content // .content_consulted // \\\"\\\" | tostring)] | @tsv' references_provenance.json | head -32; rg -n 'support|fail|unscor|attempt_03|cycle03' paper_evidence_audit_packet.md | head -25\"","aggregated_output":"This target set is constant across methods and budgets. Therefore a lower loss cannot arise merely from querying and removing difficult target cells. The history contains no rows from the corresponding held-out partition, and new-model observations never update fitted historical state.\n\nThe design combines a global model with a conditional residual estimator. Global factors share information even when direct benchmark overlap is limited. Local donor profiles can represent deviations from the global fit, but missing anchors weaken neighborhood identification. We therefore use overlap-aware weights, donor-excluded predictions, effective-count shrinkage, and historical validation with a null correction. These are safeguards in the estimator, not evidence that all remaining bias or leakage is eliminated.\n\n\\subsection{Global fit and benchmark acquisition}\nThe historical mean $\\mu_j$ is computed from observed entries in column $j$, with a neutral midpoint fallback if none is observed. Rank-$r$ row vectors $u_i$ and column vectors $v_j$ are fitted by alternating ridge solves for\n\\begin{equation}\n \\min_{U,V}\\sum_{(i,j)\\in\\Omega}(Y_{ij}-\\mu_j-u_i^\\top v_j)^2\n       +\\lambda\\left(\\sum_i\\|u_i\\|_2^2+\\sum_j\\|v_j\\|_2^2\\right).\n \\label{eq:als}\n\\subsection{Cross-fitted local residual transfer}\nHistorical donors are partitioned into folds. For each donor fold, an auxiliary factor model is fitted without those donors. A donor's available entries among $Q$ condition that model using Equation~\\ref{eq:condition}; its finite observed target entries give residuals $e_{ij}(Q)=Y_{ij}-b^{(-i)}_{ij}(Q)$. The superscript denotes fold exclusion, not a separate fit for every donor. An unobserved donor target contributes no residual. Auxiliary predictions and the main base prediction are clipped before residual calculation or adjustment.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/selected/src/program.py#L97-L142]]\n\nFor donor $i$, let $A_i=Q\\cap\\Omega_i$ be its shared queried anchors and $s_{ia}$ the standard deviation of column $a$ in that donor's auxiliary fit, floored at 0.025. At bandwidth $h$, the profile distance and weight are\n\\begin{equation}\nDisrupting historical column alignment removes the advantage over column means, and the comparisons do not isolate a residual-specific causal effect. Observed-cell prediction with heuristic family separation does not establish recovery of genuinely missing scores, transfer to generative models, or a generally sufficient budget. The finding is a configuration-level advantage with an explicit failure boundary: historical score structure can support compact evaluation, but its suitability must be examined rather than assumed.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{iclr2026_conference}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\begingroup\n\\renewcommand{\\thesection}{}\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\\endgroup\n\\thispagestyle{plain}\n\\subsection{Observed-score extraction and split provenance}\nThe final data manifest records selection of a modal supported dataset revision, split, and metric per task/subset with at least 32 rows globally, followed by a maximal-coverage model revision and a minimum of 12 cells per retained row. Normalized task/version/score content is canonicalized using Unicode normalization, whitespace normalization, sorted keys, and integral-number canonicalization while excluding source identifiers and timing. The recorded allocation uses family lexical-lineage heuristics and document/row duplicate closure, then assigns components to disjoint development and final partitions. The final evaluator re-extracts scores from retained source documents and checks content integrity, alias overlap, and split constraints. These checks are inspectable in the accompanying source; the fingerprints alone do not substantiate extraction semantics.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/resources/final/manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L666-L770]]\n\n8089 & 0.06262 & 0.23947 & 0.04751 & 0.04596 & 0.04594 & 0.04113 \\\\\n9011 & 0.07063 & 0.23404 & 0.05142 & 0.04959 & 0.04815 & 0.04280 \\\\\n\\midrule\nSeed SD & 0.00575 & 0.00278 & 0.00307 & 0.00339 & 0.00330 & 0.00217 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Sealed-target MAE averaged equally over primary holdouts and budgets within each seed; SD is descriptive sample standard deviation. Shared source data preclude interpreting these as independent dataset replications.}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe baseline-specific component-bootstrap bounds, in the order column mean, query mean, random low-rank, variance low-rank, and linear ridge, are respectively $[0.01538,0.04037]$, $[0.18530,0.20768]$, $[0.00412,0.01426]$, $[0.00340,0.00920]$, and $[0.00210,0.00939]$. They bound baseline-minus-studied errors under the retained construction. Taking lower and upper minima separately produces the reported pair; it does not select a single baseline interval. The evaluator's zero-weight-split convention inserts a conservative negative gain for such a bootstrap split. The reproduction script preserves that convention rather than silently dropping the split.\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/baseline_gain_bounds]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L999-L1017]]\n\n\\subsection{Changing-target diagnostic and disruption interpretation}\nThe evaluator also records loss over all observed but currently unqueried cells. Seed- and budget-averaged values for the studied package are 0.03665 on model holdout, 0.04242 on family holdout, 0.06528 with constant history, and 0.06352 with shifted history. This diagnostic changes its evaluated cell set as acquisitions accumulate and can also differ across query policies. It is therefore not used for the principal comparative claim. In particular, its shifted-history value cannot be substituted for the sealed-target control result.\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/changing_target_mae]]\n\nIn the constant control, all methods using only centered historical linear variation collapse to the same column means on sealed targets in the retained arrays. The query-mean reference remains different because it uses real new-model query values rather than the collapsed historical values. Column shifting preserves each column's observed scores and count, including the value/mask pairing, but not the row-wise alignment. These controls distinguish information in per-column distributions from information in cross-column relationships at the package level; they do not isolate a correction mechanism or prove that every relationship has been destroyed.\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/cases/C/mean]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluate_final.py#L808-L830]]\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility and incomplete evidence}\nThe accompanying \\texttt{README.md} maps source files, commands, dependencies, and data versions. Readable snapshots preserve the evaluated estimator, the original predictor, and the final evaluator. Figures and numeric tables are generated by \\texttt{build\\_paper\\_artifacts.py} from the immutable evidence registry, using NumPy for array computations and Matplotlib for quantitative plots \\citep{Harris2020,Hunter2007}. The conceptual overview is a qualitative diagram with separate rendering provenance. No schematic numeric values are used as experimental results. Every quantitative display has an input trail; source locations support implementation claims separately from file fingerprints.\n\nDevelopment selection was adaptive and preceded freezing the final configuration. Two development configurations had numeric scores; a support-gating configuration had no retained numeric score after evaluation failure. The failure diagnostics do not identify whether that failure was statistical, implementation-related, or due to another condition. It is missing performance evidence, not a measured loss, and neither development comparison is a matched final ablation. This paper uses the independent final evaluation for its quantitative comparisons. No additional ablation, temporal holdout, missingness stratification, or cross-leaderboard study was completed for the manuscript.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/all_programs]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/result.total_evaluated_programs]]\n\nRetrieved reference metadata and consulted abstracts are retained locally with provenance links. The literature discussion is limited to that consulted content and does not claim an exhaustive search or establish priority.\n\n\\subsection{LLM Usage}\nLanguage models assisted with research hypothesis formulation, implementation drafting, reference-query planning, manuscript framing, writing, and evidence/validation code. The paper-writing assistant inspected the retained implementation and evaluator, generated deterministic summaries and plots from retained arrays, and revised the manuscript against compilation and grounding checks. The numerical model-evaluation evidence comes from the frozen evaluator, not language-model-generated measurements. Language-model assistance does not provide independent verification of novelty or statistical validity. The conceptual-figure workflow may use model-assisted rendering; this does not introduce additional empirical evidence.\n\n\\end{document}\nMTEB\tMTEB: Massive Text Embedding Benchmark\tabstract\nTinyBenchmarks\ttinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples\tabstract\nMetaBench\tmetabench -- A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models\tabstract\nObservationalScaling\tObservational Scaling Laws and the Predictability of Langauge Model Performance\tabstract\nSloth\tSloth: scaling laws for LLM skills to predict multi-benchmark performance across families\tabstract\nKoren2009\tMatrix Factorization Techniques for Recommender Systems\tabstract\nHu2008\tCollaborative Filtering for Implicit Feedback Datasets\tabstract\nCandes2009\tExact Matrix Completion via Convex Optimization\tabstract\nJain2013\tLow-rank Matrix Completion using Alternating Minimization\tabstract\nRennie2005\tFast maximum margin matrix factorization for collaborative prediction\tabstract\nKoenker2001\tQuantile Regression\tabstract\nStone1977\tConsistent Nonparametric Regression\tabstract\nBengio2004\tNo Unbiased Estimator of the Variance of K-Fold Cross-Validation\tabstract\nHoerl1970\tRidge Regression: Biased Estimation for Nonorthogonal Problems\tabstract\nChaloner1995\tBayesian Experimental Design: A Review\tabstract\nCohn1996\tActive Learning with Statistical Models\tabstract\nRubin1976\tInference and Missing Data\tabstract\nEfron1979\tBootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife\tabstract\nCameron2008\tBootstrap-Based Improvements for Inference with Clustered Errors\tabstract\nTakacs2008\tMatrix factorization and neighbor based algorithms for the netflix prize problem\tabstract\nMnih2007\tProbabilistic Matrix Factorization\tabstract\nSalakhutdinov2008\tBayesian probabilistic matrix factorization using Markov chain Monte Carlo\tabstract\nCover1967\tNearest neighbor pattern classification\tabstract\nHarris2020\tArray programming with NumPy\tabstract\nHunter2007\tMatplotlib: A 2D Graphics Environment\tabstract\n12:- claims grounded to the wrong source ids, or source ids that do not support the visible wording\n89:      \"claim\": \"English text-only main-score extraction preserves bounded noncorrelations and maps correlations by (x+1)/2; task-definition revision 5a83aafe4e7140d0606b96f59448c704f31dbf76, global support 32 and row support 12 are manifest rules.\",\n154:      \"claim\": \"Weighted median residual correction uses effective count (sum w)^2/max(sum(w^2),1e-12) in prediction and calibration, excludes queried columns, clips to [0,1], and leaves the base unchanged at total weight at most 1e-12 or at zero blend. Effective count measures concentration only when the denominator floor is inactive; an active floor introduces absolute-weight-scale dependence without certifying geometric support.\",\n368:      \"claim\": \"No matched final residual-off, unconditional-residual, donor-only, support-gating, temporal, cross-leaderboard or missingness-stratified result is available; sequential development does not isolate causal components.\",\n379:      \"claim\": \"Two development configurations have numeric results; one support-gating evaluation has no retained numeric performance and an unknown failure cause. It is not a measured loss.\",\n797:Predicting an embedding model's benchmark profile from a small evaluation budget requires both selecting informative benchmarks and transferring information from incomplete historical results. We study a low-rank predictor augmented with cross-fitted, locally weighted residual medians, evaluated on a pinned public MTEB score matrix. The predictor chooses queries by integrated variance reduction and calibrates residual transfer using historical models only. Across model and heuristic-family holdouts, three statistical seeds, and budgets of one, two, four, and eight queried task/subset columns, its aggregate mean absolute error is 0.04081, compared with 0.04525 for the strongest aggregate baseline. Error decreases from 0.04523 at one query to 0.03615 at eight. Historical-data disruptions delimit this finding: independent column shifts remove the advantage over column means. These results support the evaluated configuration on previously observed leaderboard cells, not causal attribution to residual transfer, prediction of genuinely missing results, or a universally sufficient evaluation budget.\n814:Our contributions are a fully specified budgeted predictor, a fixed-target evaluation with dependence-aware paired uncertainty, and a measured failure boundary. Factorization, local weighting, and design have established antecedents; we do not claim novelty for these primitives or optimality of the complete configuration.\n819:\\caption{Information boundaries of budgeted score prediction. Historical scores support global factor fitting and cross-fitted donor calibration. A held-out model supplies only queried scores; these condition its latent representation and local donor weights. Sealed evaluation targets are never passed to the predictor. Query indices depend on fitted factors and earlier indices, not on the revealed score values. The diagram describes the evaluated computation, not evidence of a component-specific benefit.}\n892:The weighted median is the smallest sorted residual whose cumulative weight reaches half the total. It minimizes weighted absolute residual loss, but shrinkage and clipping need not preserve that minimizer. Total weight at most $10^{-12}$ leaves the base unchanged; the separate denominator floor in Equation~\\ref{eq:correction} applies in prediction and calibration. Queried entries are not residual-adjusted. Without an active denominator floor, effective count measures weight concentration; when active, absolute weight scale also matters. Neither case certifies geometric support or extrapolation: equally weighted distant donors can still have a large count when the floor is inactive.\n906:The retained final matrix has 126 rows and 135 columns, where a column identifies task, dataset revision, split, subset, and metric. These are not 135 independent benchmark suites. Extraction retains supported English text-only main metrics, checks their agreement with the reported main score, and chooses a common supported revision/metric/split per task/subset under the recorded coverage rule. Per-model revision selection favors coverage. Bounded noncorrelation scores remain in $[0,1]$; correlations are mapped by $(x+1)/2$. Hence errors are in normalized score units, not percentage-point errors uniformly comparable across raw metrics.\n913:For each new-model row, the observed columns are shuffled deterministically and divided into a query reservoir containing approximately two thirds and a sealed remainder. The cut is at least eight columns, and each retained row has enough support for a nonempty target set. Targets and the complete target mask are withheld. The predictor receives an opaque indexed historical matrix, the queryable indices, and only the scores it requests. It cannot query genuinely missing cells in this experiment. Thus the study is a retrospective prediction test conditional on historical reporting, not evidence that unreported leaderboard scores can be recovered without selection bias.\n954:The package's seed-level pooled MAEs are 0.03850, 0.04113, and 0.04280 (sample SD 0.00217), versus linear ridge's 0.04166, 0.04594, and 0.04815. These shared-data replications all favor the package. Component-bootstrap bound minima are $[0.00210,0.00920]$. Their positive lower minimum supports the aggregate comparisons under the specified construction; the endpoints need not correspond to the same baseline.\n992:The constant condition is consistent with an estimator that falls back to column-level information when between-model variation vanishes. The shifted condition shows that preserving per-column value distributions is insufficient to retain the package's advantage. Neither observation identifies residual transfer as the cause: shifts change the factor fit, acquisition order, donor neighborhoods, and calibration simultaneously. Likewise, failure after disruption is not evidence that the intact-history result was invalid. It establishes a measured boundary under these particular matched transformations.\n995:\\paragraph{Prediction support and missingness.}\n999:There is no matched outer comparison that removes only residual correction while preserving all other fitting and acquisition choices. Historical tuning diagnostics are not substitutes for such an ablation. The finite donor bank, short auxiliary fits, and limited shared anchors can induce residual estimation error; fold exclusion does not ensure residual unbiasedness. Effective-count shrinkage reflects concentration and, with an active denominator floor, absolute weight scale, but does not certify similarity. A history-chosen null blend cannot guarantee protection against a shifted new-model distribution. The observed control failure qualifies the predictor's usefulness without establishing a mechanism.\n1005:The evaluated configuration was frozen after development comparisons, so those comparisons are not independent confirmation or controlled component tests. A separate support-gating variant did not yield a retained numeric performance result; its failure cause is not diagnosed and cannot be counted as a measured loss. The final qualification passed, but this does not erase selection uncertainty or the limitations above. For deployment, the appropriate fallback depends on uncertainty about historical comparability, not only the intact-matrix aggregate. The current evidence motivates testing such decisions; it does not validate a switching rule or establish end-to-end savings.\n1027:The final data manifest records selection of a modal supported dataset revision, split, and metric per task/subset with at least 32 rows globally, followed by a maximal-coverage model revision and a minimum of 12 cells per retained row. Normalized task/version/score content is canonicalized using Unicode normalization, whitespace normalization, sorted keys, and integral-number canonicalization while excluding source identifiers and timing. The recorded allocation uses family lexical-lineage heuristics and document/row duplicate closure, then assigns components to disjoint development and final partitions. The final evaluator re-extracts scores from retained source documents and checks content integrity, alias overlap, and split constraints. These checks are inspectable in the accompanying source; the fingerprints alone do not substantiate extraction semantics.\n1060:For each validation fold, remove its rows from the historical fitting pool. Fit the auxiliary base on the remaining rows, select a donor pool from those rows, and split donors into two folds. For each donor fold, fit without that fold, retaining only observed donor scores. For a validation row, simulate the same acquisition criterion on its available scores. Cache donor-excluded predictions for each queried index tuple; the cache affects computation but not access to targets. At each reporting budget, evaluate the clipped base plus each candidate correction on finite unqueried validation cells. Pool absolute-error sums and target counts across these cells and folds, select bandwidth/shrinkage/blend, and rebuild the donor bank from historical data. If support checks fail, retain the zero-blend configuration. Auxiliary randomness uses a separate seed sequence, preserving the main-fit random stream.\n1124:The accompanying \\texttt{README.md} maps source files, commands, dependencies, and data versions. Readable snapshots preserve the evaluated estimator, the original predictor, and the final evaluator. Figures and numeric tables are generated by \\texttt{build\\_paper\\_artifacts.py} from the immutable evidence registry, using NumPy for array computations and Matplotlib for quantitative plots \\citep{Harris2020,Hunter2007}. The conceptual overview is a qualitative diagram with separate rendering provenance. No schematic numeric values are used as experimental results. Every quantitative display has an input trail; source locations support implementation claims separately from file fingerprints.\n1126:Development selection was adaptive and preceded freezing the final configuration. Two development configurations had numeric scores; a support-gating configuration had no retained numeric score after evaluation failure. The failure diagnostics do not identify whether that failure was statistical, implementation-related, or due to another condition. It is missing performance evidence, not a measured loss, and neither development comparison is a matched final ablation. This paper uses the independent final evaluation for its quantitative comparisons. No additional ablation, temporal holdout, missingness stratification, or cross-leaderboard study was completed for the manuscript.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
